Debug LinxInit
---------------

 LinxInit is a firmware for Linx machine, it is based on Linx block ISA, its URL
 is https://codehub-dg-y.huawei.com/LinxISAV150/LinxInit.git. You can build it
 and use -bios LinxInit.bin to run it.

 LinxInit is the default firmware for Linx qemu virt machine, so if there is no
 -bios, qemu/pc-bios/LinxInit.bin will be used.

 A LinxInit submodule has been added into this qemu repo, so you can follow
 below steps to download、build and replace LinxInit.bin in pc-bios:

  1. cd qemu
  2. git clone --recurse https://codehub-dg-y.huawei.com/LinxISAV150/LinxInit.git ./roms/LinxInit/
  3. cd qemu/roms
  4. make linx CROSS_COMPILE=<your_cross_compile_toolchain_path>

Qemu debug tools
-----------------

 Qemu already offers to debug options by -d parameter. You can use
 "qemu-linx -d help" to get related document.

 We also add some debug options:

 1. -linx_debug [pc_stop=address][,pc_count_begin=addr][,max_insn=insn_number]

    e.g. "-linx_debug pc_stop=0x80200100" will stop qemu in 0x80200100, and you
         can enter into qemu monitor to see the value of registers and memory.

    e.g. "-linx_debug pc_count_begin=0x80200100,max_insn=2000" will stop qemu
         in 0x80200100 + 2000 instructions.

 2. A debug instruction like below: 

     31                                           3 2             0
    +----------------------------------------------+---------------+
    |                                              |     111       |
    +----------------------------------------------+---------------+

     63                                                           0
    +--------------------------------------------------------------+
    |                          res[63:0]                           |
    +--------------------------------------------------------------+

     31                                          10 9             0
    +----------------------------------------------+---------------+
    |                                              |  battr[9:0]   |
    +----------------------------------------------+---------------+


    use block head instruction, the head opcode is 0b0000001011, battr[9] is 
    set and battr[8..0] is mean as below:

    0x001 STOP_VM, stop qemu and can use monitor to diagnose.

    0x002 DUMP_STATE, print CPU registers.

    0x004 DUMP_MEM, print the r10(a0) register in decimal format with unsigned long, and
          use r11(a1) as the start address of the memory to be dumped and r12(a2) as the
          size of memory, and then, the memory value will be printed.

    0x008 SHOW_ID, print the r10(a0) register in decimal format with unsigned long.

    0x010 DUMP_STRING, print the r10(a0) register in decimal format with unsigned long, and
          print the memory to which r11(a1) points as a string.

    Note: DUMP_MEM, SHOW_ID, DUMP_STRING are exclusive. That is, each debug instruction can
    use only one of them. For example, if SHOW_ID is used, DUMP_MEM cannot be used. But
    STOP_VM and DUMP_STATE can work with other battr.

    Except DUMP_MEM, SHOW_ID, and DUMP_STRING, other battr (defined & undefined) will print
    instruction codes, in this case, 'res' in the debug instruction can be used as a data to
    be printed.

 3. --trace "linx_*"

    currently we have linx_head/linx_mini to trace head/mini translation.

 4. -symfile

    Specifying symbols manually from vmlinux.
    It needs to be used with "-d in_asm"

    e.g. "-d in_asm -symfile your_kernel_path/vmlinux "

 3. extension on "-d mmu"

    Now "-d mmu" can output Page Walk log.
    E.g:

    page table SV39 walk level 0: pte(82604010)=0

 5. breakpoint feature in TCG

    A TCG breakpoint is an condition checker of TCG. When the condition is
    matched in TCG execution, The breakpoint is hit and some action will be
    taken.

    Breakpoints can be added by qemu command line options or monitor
    command. E.g.:

    1. qemu -tcgbp spec,spec,...

    2. qemu> tcgbp add <cpu> spec

    Spec is the breakpoint specification in format "[attr]addr+opt". Attr is
    the attributes of the breakpoint. It can be combination of:

    R: Read
    W: Write
    O: One-shot
    N: Non-stop

    address is the breakpoint's address

    opt is an number as parameter of the breakpoint.

    There are these types of breakpoint:

    1. pc, it is hit when a translation block pass the PC. This is a spec
    example: 0x80000873

    2. counter pc, it is hit when a pc is pass and more than *count*
    instruction is consumed by further translation block. These are some spec
    example: 0x80000874+1000, 0x80000874+0x3000

    3. data, it is hit when a translation block touch the target memory.
    These are some spec example: R0x80000000+100, RWN0x80009000. If the size
    is not specified, it is set to default of 8. Any memory operation
    touch the range is taken as a "touch".

    When the breakpoint is hit. Its hit counter is added and the vm is stop
    if the Non-stop attribute is not set.

    monitor commands can be used to add, remove, disable, enable and list
    all current breakpont per cpu. All these commands are in form of

        [tcgbp|tb] [subcmd] [cpu-spec] [spec]

    These some example:

    1. tb list * all: list all breakpoint for all cpu
    2. tb add 1 0x80000900: add a pc breakpoint
    3. tb remove 0 1: remove breakpoint 1 from cpu 0

    watchout:

    1. After a TB executes, qemu would check out breakpoints
    2. You should set a pointbreak on block's body instead of block's head.
